/**
 * The home page.
 *
 * It was `/`, the alternative to a homepage that argued by sequence. That
 * one is retired — kept in the repo as `_retired-home.astro`, which Astro does
 * not build, so it is recoverable without being reachable.
 *
 * * It leads with one console carrying a whole wallet's standing authority, and
 * runs the control loop across it: read, decide, act, settle, verify.
 *
 * The table shows ALL SIX readings the engine can emit (src/lib/readings.js)
 * across nine chains, because both halves are the claim. A wallet is not one
 * chain and a permission is not one state, and a table showing two readings on
 * two chains argues for a much smaller product than this one.
 *
 *   UNBOUNDED  Ethereum   Uniswap Permit2    -> limited on screen
 *   UNBOUNDED  Ethereum   OpenSea Seaport    -> revoked on screen
 *   OVER_WIDE  Arbitrum   GMX Router          finite, but bigger than the balance
 *   BOUNDED    Base       Aerodrome Router    capped and dated
 *   UNKNOWN    Linea      SyncSwap Router     the contract would not answer
 *   EXPIRED    Polygon    1inch Router        still set, and reaches nothing
 *   REMOVED    BNB Chain  PancakeSwap Router  revoked earlier
 *   BOUNDED    Optimism   Velodrome Router
 *   BOUNDED    Avalanche  Trader Joe Router
 *   BOUNDED    Scroll     Ambient Router
 *
 * Three of these do work no competitor's table does.
 *
 * OVER_WIDE is the reading almost nobody has: finite, so it looks fine, but
 * larger than the balance it applies to, so deposits not yet made are covered
 * too. A tool that only flags "unlimited" reports this one as safe.
 *
 * EXPIRED is deliberately distinct from REMOVED: 1,200 is still set on chain
 * and reaches nothing, which is exactly the case a reader misjudges when a
 * tool reports allowances without reporting expiry.
 *
 * UNKNOWN is the honest one, and it is here on purpose. The contract did not
 * answer the calls needed to read it, so we say so rather than rendering it as
 * clean — and DECISION §75 forbids offering a correction for it, because
 * narrowing an authority we could not read is how false confidence starts.
 * Showing the gap is the credibility.
 *
 * THE COUNTERPARTIES ARE REAL AND NAMING THEM IS NOT AN ACCUSATION. An
 * unlimited ERC-20 approval to Uniswap's Permit2, or setApprovalForAll to
 * OpenSea's Seaport, is the ordinary way these applications work and is
 * granted by the user, not taken. Inventing ExampleSwap implied the problem
 * belongs to disreputable software; it does not, and the whole thesis is that
 * standing authority is the norm rather than the exception. The figures
 * against them are invented, the wallet is marked an example, and nothing
 * here says any of these applications has done anything wrong.
 *
 * VIEW ONLY. Nothing in the table is interactive — it is a depiction of a
 * product that is not shipped yet, and controls that did nothing would be
 * worse than none.
 *
 * Ships no JavaScript, like `/`.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../data/site.js';
import ChainStack from '../components/ChainStack.jsx';
import ConsoleTable from '../components/sections/ConsoleTable.jsx';
import Stats from '../components/sections/Stats.jsx';
import HowItWorksDeck from '../components/sections/HowItWorksDeck.astro';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.jsx';
import Developers from '../components/sections/Developers.jsx';
/* theme.css only, and only for its Tailwind utilities: the asset marks in the
   console below are drawn by ui/TokenMark.jsx, which is written in them. The
   ledger's own layers — dashboard.css and demo.css — went back to /demo with
   the ledger. */
import '../styles/theme.css';
import '../styles/home.css';
import '../styles/alt.css';

/* Three wallets, cycled. A table that always shows the same rows with the same
   amounts is a screenshot: watch it twice and you know what comes next, which
   is the opposite of how a product reading live chain state should feel.
   Each wallet gets the same twelve seconds and the same beats, because the loop
   is the constant — only what it is looking at changes.

   Every header total is the sum of its own reachable column:
     set 1   8,420 + 1,920 + 6,180 + 2,500 + 500 + 1,250 + 340   =  21,110
     set 2  a treasury: 1,240,000 + 386,000 + 410,000 + 180,000
            + 64,000 + 95,500 + 22,300                          = 2,397,800
     set 3   2,150 +   380 +  1,900 +   750 + 300 + 1,100 + 180  =   6,760
   and each falls by exactly what its limit and its revoke remove. */
